  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before patterns and problems, let's understand&nbsp;<strong>what kind of math actually shows up</strong>&nbsp;in coding interviews and why it matters.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-why-maths-in-coding-interviews",
      "title": "1. Why Maths in Coding Interviews?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Most coding problems have a brute force solution. Maths lets you skip the brute force entirely.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280773-553697124.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Brute force sums numbers from 1 to n in&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(n)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;time.</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Using the formula&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">n × (n + 1) / 2</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;computes it in&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">O(1)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;time.</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n        total += i;\n    }\n\n    cout << total << endl;\n    return 0;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Knowing the right mathematical property turns an O(n) or O(n²) solution into a one-liner.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-1-number-theory",
      "title": "1.1 Number Theory",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The foundation of most math problems in interviews.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "1. Divisibility",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Divisibility rules provide quick ways to check if a number is divisible by another without performing full division. These rules are based on patterns in digits, like sum of digits or last digit.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A number&nbsp;<code>a</code>&nbsp;is divisible by&nbsp;<code>b</code>&nbsp;if dividing leaves no remainder.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280774-871685375.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280774-871685375.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280774-871685375.png",
          "alt": "Divisibility"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Factors and Multiples:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Factors of 12:   1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12\n                 (numbers that divide 12 evenly)\n\nMultiples of 4:  4, 8, 12, 16, 20 ...\n                 (numbers 4 divides into evenly)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "2. Prime Numbers",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Prime numbers are those that are divisible only by 1 and themselves. They form the foundation of number theory because every number can be broken into primes, making them essential for factorization and many algorithms.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A number greater than 1 that has&nbsp;<strong>no factors other than 1 and itself</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280774-527711729.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280774-527711729.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280774-527711729.png",
          "alt": "Prime Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Checking if a number is prime:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You only need to check divisors up to √n. If no divisor found by then, it's prime.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To check if&nbsp;<strong>37 is prime</strong>, we only need to test divisors up to its square root.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "√37 ≈ 6.08 → so we check integers from&nbsp;<strong>2 to 6</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "37 ÷ 2 = 18.5 → not divisible",
            "37 ÷ 3 = 12.33 → not divisible",
            "37 ÷ 4 = not divisible",
            "37 ÷ 5 = 7.4 → not divisible",
            "37 ÷ 6 = not divisible"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Since none of these divide 37 exactly, it has&nbsp;<strong>no divisors other than 1 and itself</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Therefore,&nbsp;<strong>37 is a prime number ✓</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "boolean isPrime(int n) {\n    if (n < 2) return false;\n    for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {\n        if (n % i == 0) return false;\n    }\n    return true;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Prime number check (single number):</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Time:</strong>&nbsp;O(√n)<br><strong>Space:</strong>&nbsp;O(1)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Why time is O(√n):</strong><br>If a number&nbsp;<strong>n</strong>&nbsp;has a factor greater than √n, then it must also have a factor smaller than √n.<br>So checking divisibility up to √n is sufficient no need to go till n.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Why space is O(1):</strong><br>We only use a few variables, no extra memory.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "3. Sieve of Eratosthenes",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "The sieve is an efficient method to find all primes up to N by iteratively removing multiples of each prime. Instead of checking each number individually, it eliminates non-primes in bulk, achieving O(n log log n) time.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find&nbsp;<strong>all prime numbers up to N</strong>&nbsp;efficiently.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280774-394650011.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Example: N = 20</strong><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Start by assuming all numbers from&nbsp;<strong>2 to 20 are prime</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 1: 2 is prime</strong><br>Cross out all multiples of 2:<br>4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 2: 3 is prime</strong><br>Cross out all multiples of 3:<br>6, 9, 12, 15, 18",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 3: 4 is already crossed out → skip</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Step 4: 5 is prime</strong><br>Cross out multiples of 5:<br>10, 15, 20",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Stop at&nbsp;<strong>√20 ≈ 4.4</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Remaining numbers (primes):</strong><br>2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "boolean[] sieve(int n) {\n    boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[n + 1];\n    Arrays.fill(isPrime, true);\n    isPrime[0] = isPrime[1] = false;\n\n    for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {\n        if (isPrime[i]) {\n            for (int j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {\n                isPrime[j] = false;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return isPrime;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Time:</strong>&nbsp;O(n log log n)<br><strong>Space:</strong>&nbsp;O(n)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Why time is O(n log log n):</strong><br>Each prime&nbsp;<strong>p</strong>&nbsp;eliminates its multiples (p, 2p, 3p, …). The total work is roughly:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "n × (1/2 + 1/3 + 1/5 + 1/7 + … over primes)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This sum grows like&nbsp;<strong>log log n</strong>, so total complexity becomes&nbsp;<strong>O(n log log n)</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Why space is O(n):</strong><br>We maintain an array of size n to mark whether each number is prime or not.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "4. GCD and LCM",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "GCD is the largest number that divides two numbers without leaving a remainder. Using the Euclidean algorithm, we repeatedly reduce the problem using remainders, making it very fast even for large numbers.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>GCD (Greatest Common Divisor)</strong>&nbsp;the largest number that divides both numbers.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280775-480727744.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Example:</strong><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "GCD(12, 8)<br>12 = 2 × 2 × 3<br>8 = 2 × 2 × 2<br>Common factors → 2 × 2 = 4<br><strong>GCD = 4</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Euclidean Algorithm (fast method):</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "GCD(a, b) = GCD(b, a % b)<br>GCD(a, 0) = a",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Example:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "GCD(48, 18)<br>→ GCD(18, 12)<br>→ GCD(12, 6)<br>→ GCD(6, 0) = 6",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "int gcd(int a, int b) {\n    return b == 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b);\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "LCM (Least Common Multiple)&nbsp;&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "LCM is the smallest number that is divisible by both given numbers. It is closely related to GCD and can be computed efficiently using the formula (a × b) / GCD(a, b).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "the smallest number divisible by both numbers.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280775-879636287.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">LCM(a, b) = (a × b) / GCD(a, b)</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Example:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "LCM(4, 6) = (4 × 6) / GCD(4, 6)&nbsp;= 24 / 2 = 12",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-2-modular-arithmetic",
      "title": "1.2 Modular Arithmetic",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Modular arithmetic works with remainders to keep numbers within limits, especially when dealing with very large values. It allows operations like addition and multiplication to be performed safely under a modulus.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280775-288613776.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">When numbers become very large, results are often taken&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">modulo m</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;(commonly&nbsp;109+7109+7) to keep them manageable.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Key properties:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">(a + b) % m = (a % m + b % m) % m</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">(a × b) % m = (a % m × b % m) % m</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">(a − b) % m = (a % m − b % m + m) % m&nbsp;</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>(+ m ensures the result is non-negative)</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Why 10^9 + 7 specifically? It is a prime number just under int overflow range &nbsp;useful for modular inverse calculations.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "int MOD = 1_000_000_007;\nlong result = (a % MOD * b % MOD) % MOD;"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "1. Fast Power (Binary Exponentiation)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Fast exponentiation computes powers in logarithmic time by repeatedly squaring the base and reducing the exponent. This avoids the inefficiency of multiplying the number n times.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Compute&nbsp;<strong>aⁿ mod m</strong>&nbsp;efficiently.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280775-580315618.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Brute force:</strong><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Multiply a by itself n times →&nbsp;<strong>O(n)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Fast power idea:</strong><br>Repeatedly split the exponent in half.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "a⁸ = (a⁴)²<br>a⁴ = (a²)²<br>a² = (a¹)²",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So instead of n multiplications, you do about&nbsp;<strong>log n steps</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "long power(long base, long exp, long mod) {\n    long result = 1;\n    base %= mod;\n\n    while (exp > 0) {\n        if (exp % 2 == 1)\n            result = result * base % mod;   // odd exponent\n        base = base * base % mod;           // square the base\n        exp /= 2;\n    }\n    return result;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Time:</strong>&nbsp;O(log n)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "<hr style=\"border-width: 1px medium medium; border-style: solid none none; border-color: rgb(224, 224, 224) currentcolor currentcolor; border-image: none; margin: 24px 0px;\">",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-3-combinatorics",
      "title": "1.3 Combinatorics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Counting arrangements and selections.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406280776-895694282.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "<span style=\"font-size: 1.75rem; letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">1.&nbsp;Permutations vs Combinations</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>1. Permutation order matters</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Number of ways to arrange r items from n:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "P(n, r) = n! / (n − r)!",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Choosing 3 people from 5 for positions (1st, 2nd, 3rd):<br>P(5, 3) = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>2. Combination: order does not matter</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Number of ways to choose r items from n:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "C(n, r) = n! / (r! × (n − r)!)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Example:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Choosing 3 people from 5 for a team:<br>C(5, 3) = 10",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "2. Pascal’s Triangle",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A fast way to compute combinations without using factorials.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367107-554514130.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Each value is formed using:</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "C(row, col) = C(row−1, col−1) + C(row−1, col)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Example:</strong><br>C(4, 2) = 6 (4th row, 2nd column)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "int[][] pascal(int n) {\n    int[][] C = new int[n+1][n+1];\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {\n        C[i][0] = 1;\n        for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {\n            C[i][j] = C[i-1][j-1] + C[i-1][j];\n        }\n    }\n    return C;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-4-geometry",
      "title": "1.4 Geometry",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Common geometric concepts used in interviews.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "1. Distance Between Two Points",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Points: (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Distance = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Example:</strong><br>Points (1, 2) and (4, 6)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Distance = √((4 − 1)² + (6 − 2)²)<br>     = √(9 + 16)<br>     = √25 = 5",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "double distance(int x1, int y1, int x2, int y2) {\n    return Math.sqrt(Math.pow(x2-x1, 2) + Math.pow(y2-y1, 2));\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Use&nbsp;<strong>squared distance&nbsp;</strong>when you only need to compare distances it avoids square roots and floating-point errors.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "2. Slope of a Line",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "slope = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Example:</strong><br>Points (1, 2) and (3, 6)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "slope = (6 − 2) / (3 − 1) = 4 / 2 = 2",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Key relationships:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Two lines are&nbsp;<strong>parallel</strong>&nbsp;if their slopes are equal",
            "Two lines are&nbsp;<strong>perpendicular</strong>&nbsp;if:<br>m₁ × m₂ = −1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "In interviews, to avoid floating point, compare slopes as fractions:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "// instead of dy1/dx1 == dy2/dx2\n// compare dy1 * dx2 == dy2 * dx1"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "3. Area of a Triangle",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Given three points: (x₁, y₁), (x₂, y₂), (x₃, y₃)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Area = |x₁(y₂ − y₃) + x₂(y₃ − y₁) + x₃(y₁ − y₂)| / 2",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "double triangleArea(int x1, int y1, int x2, int y2, int x3, int y3) {\n    return Math.abs(x1*(y2-y3) + x2*(y3-y1) + x3*(y1-y2)) / 2.0;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If the computed area is&nbsp;<strong>0</strong>, the three points are&nbsp;<strong>collinear</strong>(they lie on the same straight line).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "4. Cross Product ( Point Orientation )",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The cross product helps determine the&nbsp;<strong>orientation</strong>&nbsp;of three points.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-929036743.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Given three points P, Q, R:</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "cross = (Q.x − P.x) × (R.y − P.y)&nbsp;− (Q.y − P.y) × (R.x − P.x)",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Interpretation:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "cross &gt; 0 →&nbsp;<strong>counterclockwise</strong>&nbsp;(left turn)",
            "cross &lt; 0 →&nbsp;<strong>clockwise</strong>&nbsp;(right turn)",
            "cross = 0 →&nbsp;<strong>collinear</strong>&nbsp;(straight line)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "int cross(int px, int py, int qx, int qy, int rx, int ry) {\n    return (qx-px)*(ry-py) - (qy-py)*(rx-px);\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It is used extensively in&nbsp;<strong>convex hull algorithms&nbsp;</strong>and&nbsp;<strong>line intersection problems</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-common-formulas-to-know",
      "title": "2. Common Formulas to Know",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Summations:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Sum of 1 to n → n × (n + 1) / 2",
            "Sum of squares → n × (n + 1) × (2n + 1) / 6",
            "Sum of cubes → (n × (n + 1) / 2)²",
            "Geometric series → a × (rⁿ − 1) / (r − 1)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>Geometry:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Pythagorean theorem → a² + b² = c²",
            "Circle area → π × r²",
            "Circle circumference → 2 × π × r"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><strong>Key idea:</strong></p><p>Memorizing these saves time and avoids unnecessary computation in problems&nbsp;</p>",
          "items": []
        },
